        "idea": "Use scipy.optimize.minimize with SLSQP to jointly optimize all circle positions",
        "description": "Formulate the circle packing as a constrained optimization problem where we optimize all 52 position coordinates (26 circles \u00d7 2 coordinates). The objective function is the negative sum of radii (to maximize), computed from minimum distances to boundaries and other circles. Use SLSQP method with constraints: (1) each circle center stays within [r, 1-r] bounds, (2) distance between any pair of circles \u2265 sum of their radii. Start from the current hexagonal grid configuration as initial guess. Use bounds [0, 1] for all coordinates. The key innovation is allowing circles to move away from the rigid grid structure to find better local configurations that exploit edge effects and irregular spacing.",
        "what_to_optimize": "sum_radii directly by allowing positions to vary from the fixed grid pattern",
        "cautions": "Non-convex constraints make optimization challenging; use multiple random restarts from perturbed initial positions; set maxiter high enough (1000+); may need to scale the problem; constraint Jacobian computation can be expensive for 26 circles (325 pairwise constraints)",
        "approach_type": "scipy.optimize.minimize"
      },
      {
        "idea": "Use scipy.spatial.Voronoi to identify optimal neighbor structure and adjust positions accordingly",
        "description": "Instead of assuming a hexagonal grid, compute the Voronoi diagram of the current circle centers. The Voronoi cells reveal which circles are neighbors and how much 'room' each circle has. For each circle, move its center toward the centroid of its Voronoi cell to maximize local radius. Iterate this process: (1) compute Voronoi diagram using scipy.spatial.Voronoi, (2) for each circle, find its Voronoi cell, (3) move center toward cell centroid while respecting non-overlap constraints, (4) recompute radii from new positions. This data-driven approach adapts to the actual geometry rather than assuming a fixed pattern, potentially revealing asymmetric configurations that better utilize the square container.",
        "what_to_optimize": "local radius maximization for each circle based on actual neighbor geometry",
        "cautions": "Voronoi cells may be unbounded for circles near boundaries; need to clip to unit square; iteration may converge to local optimum; handle degenerate cases where points are collinear; scipy.spatial.Voronoi can be unstable for certain configurations",
        "approach_type": "scipy.spatial.Voronoi"
      },
      {
